    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm having some trouble with a series of walnut cabinet builds. I'm cutting dovetail joints, but I'm getting consistent splintering at the very tips of the pins. It's messing up the fit and making the joinery look messy. Any advice on how to manage the grain or what I should check with my chisels to stop this happening?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It sounds like the grain direction might be challenging. Ensure when you're cutting your dovetails, the chisel angles are oriented correctly for the direction of the grain. Using a sharp, fine-toothed Stanley knife can help in shaving off larger splinters, but be gentle to avoid crushing fine fibers. Additionally, always have your chisels properly sharpened, and use the correct cutting technique to reduce splintering. Blunting wood edges with force can cause more damage to the finish grain, so it’s best to avoid forcing the cuts. Consider using a sharp dovetail plane to achieve a smoother edge before inserting the pins.",
      "source": "target",
