    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks, I'll check the chisel angles again. I've been using a standard bevel, but maybe I should try a different angle or a more specialized tool? Also, do you think the moisture content of the walnut could be making the fibers more brittle and prone to that splintering, or is it purely a tool sharpness issue?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "An inappropriate bevel angle would indeed affect your ability to trail the chisel with a smooth result, which in turn can cause splintering if the angle is too steep. Experiment with using a dovetail beard plane – they are specifically designed to help out with fine shaving and cleaning up dovetail pin scours.\n\n\nMoisture content certainly plays a role in how much splintering you might encounter. Walnut is particularly susceptible as its grain structure reacts considerably to changes in humidity, which might not just be a general concern, but rather could be a specific issue with your pieces. Drying out some before you'll be working with it could reduce any brittleness and susceptibility to splintering. Regardless, sharpening your chisels and plane blades should always be a priority. If the grain is still troublesome even after other adjustments, you may want to experiment with different grit planes or even a pull saw with a fine-toothed blade specifically for these delicate cuts. Pay close attention to grain direction when putting your pieces together, and if necessary, lightly sanding the ends with a fine-grit sandpaper to prep the wood for a cleaner joint.",
      "source": "target",
