    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I'll try letting the wood sit in a controlled environment for a few days. One more thing—I've noticed that even when my tools are razor sharp, sometimes the wood just seems to behave unpredictably, like there's something about the internal structure of certain boards that makes them resist the cut or tear in weird ways. Is there anything else I should look for in the grain pattern itself to anticipate those difficult spots?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Wood's internal structure, which includes growth rings and variations in density, can indeed make your work more challenging. A good way to anticipate difficult spots is to examine the breadboard, which is the part of the grain where the wood's growth direction changes. Sudden changes between spring and summer or fall and winter growth can be hard to cut through cleanly. Placing a piece of frosted glass against the wood can also help you observe these growth patterns without actually marking or damaging the surface.\n\n\nUnderstanding how each cut interacts with the grain can also be beneficial. It's sometimes helpful to stack or glue together several pieces of wood, then cut them at the same time to understand how they're going to move as a unit. This way, you'll have a sense of how various movements can affect your joint and what preventative measures you can take. The more you work with redwood and other hardwoods, the better you’ll get at reading wood, which is an invaluable skill for fine woodworking. Developing a sense for it often requires a lot of patience and practice, but taking the time to learn this can drastically improve the quality of your work.",
      "source": "target",
